  "body": "What are the semantics of\n\n``` js\nstream.read()\nstream.read()\n```\n\nIf the stream contains bytes the first promise will return those bytes on the next tick. If no bytes then the first promise fulfills eventually.\n\nThe second promise can either\n- contain the same as the first promise because its in the same tick\n- contain the next set of bytes after the first promise fulfills with some bytes\n- be a thrown exception, cannot read from stream that is currently in reading state.\n\nThe first may \"make sense\" but is going to be a major pain in the ass for anyone that monkey punches `.read()` it also questions whether it should be for the same tick or be the same as the first promise up until the first promise resolves. This sounds really complex.\n\nThe second is far simpler and makes sense with the synchronous parallel, every `read()` call returns some number of bytes. It maintains the notion that a stream is a sequential set of binary data.\n\nThe third is arguably user unfriendly but is the simplest. If every stream implementation can assume that `read()` never gets called concurrently then it makes userland streams really easy to implement.\n",
